  "description": "Subtract coordinator-observed latency from client-observed latency to size the network, driver and dispatch cost. The client reports seconds and the server microseconds; every panel normalises before it compares.",
  "editable": true,
  "fiscalYearStartMonth": 0,
  "graphTooltip": 1,
  "id": null,
  "links": [],
  "liveNow": false,
  "panels": [
    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 11,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "id": 1,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "This dashboard answers one question: **is it the database, or the path to it?**\n\n`cassandra-easy-stress` measures latency at the **client**, from just before the driver sends a\nstatement to just after the response lands. Cassandra measures latency at the **coordinator**, from\nthe moment a request is decoded to the moment a reply is written. The difference is everything in\nbetween: the network, the driver queue, connection contention, and coordinator dispatch. Nobody\nsubtracts the two numbers, so nobody sees that gap.\n\n**Units, because this is the trap.** The client summary reports **seconds**\n(`selects`, `mutations`, quantile label). The server gauges report **microseconds**\n(`cassandra_client_request_latency_*_microseconds`). Every client expression on this dashboard is\nmultiplied by `1000000` before it is compared, then both sides are divided by **Latency unit**.\nSubtracting them raw is wrong by a factor of one million.\n\n**Two percentile dropdowns, on purpose.** The server family encodes the percentile in the metric\nNAME (`p50`, `p99`, `p999`, `max`); the client summary encodes it in a numeric `quantile` LABEL\n(`0.5`, `0.99`, `0.999`). One variable cannot drive both. Set **Percentile** and **Client quantile**\nto the same percentile. Every delta panel prints both in its title, so a mismatch is visible in a\nscreenshot instead of silently wrong. The client summary has **no** `max`, so `max` has no\nclient counterpart.\n\n**How to read it.** Client and server lines that track each other with a small constant offset mean\na healthy path; the database owns the latency. A delta that grows while server latency stays flat\nmeans the path, not the database: the network, the driver, or a saturated client. Check the\nthroughput row first. If client and server request rates disagree, the two halves are not describing\nthe same work and no delta below is meaningful.\n\n**Set Workload to a single stress job before reading a delta.** With several workloads running, the\nclient side is averaged across workloads with different shapes.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "title": "How to read this dashboard",
      "type": "text"
    },
